This repository is my capstone project for learning AI-native / agentic software engineering.

GitHub repository state is the only durable source of truth.

Core learning documents:

- docs/learning/master-learning-plan.md
- docs/learning/learning-process.md
- docs/learning/learning-cheatsheet.md
- docs/learning/deep-research-report.md
- docs/learning/progress.md
- docs/learning/experiments.md
- docs/learning/lessons/

When implementing learning tasks:

1. Read docs/learning/progress.md and relevant architecture/code first.
2. Read docs/learning/experiments.md when the task depends on prior experiment results.
3. Read master/process docs when module scope or workflow is relevant.
4. Read the current module lesson recap when one exists.
5. Before substantial implementation, show a short proposed implementation plan.
6. Implement only the smallest real version needed to learn/test the current concept.
7. Do not implement future roadmap modules prematurely.
8. Prefer explicit, understandable architecture over unnecessary framework abstraction.
9. Respect the Topic Chat split between learning-critical code and delegatable boilerplate/plumbing.
10. Run required tests/checks.
11. Run the requested experiment when practical.
12. After implementation, give a short code tour:
    - 3–5 main files/functions the user should personally inspect;
    - the role of each;
    - the execution flow between them.
13. Update docs/learning/progress.md with:
    - module/task;
    - what was implemented;
    - important design decisions;
    - current result;
    - failures/open questions.
14. Update docs/learning/experiments.md when an experiment was run.
15. Maintain module lesson artifacts under `docs/learning/lessons/NN-short-name/`:
    - `notes.md`: practical/personal notes, implementation nuances, commands, concrete results;
    - `theory.md`: concise 3–5 minute theory refresher with core mental model, mechanism/flow, boundaries, main failures/trade-offs, 2–4 practical observations from our work, and 3–6 takeaways;
    - optional `traces/`: representative evidence only.
16. Keep `theory.md` short. Do not duplicate large sections from master/research docs.
17. If a durable architecture/workflow decision emerges and matters to future modules, update the appropriate repository document; do not rely on chat history as durable state.
18. Do not mark a module complete merely because the code works.
19. Keep the human learning Git flow sequential on main:
    module → implementation → tests/experiment → review/understanding → lesson recap → progress update → commit → push.
20. Harness benchmark/eval runs use per-run Git worktrees (Module 08). Do not introduce extra human worktrees, complex branching, or parallel module development unless a later module requires them.
21. After the user reviews the learning-critical code and the Topic Chat closes the module, commit and push the completed module state to GitHub.
